  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nScattered showers and an isolated thunderstorm are expected\ntonight as a cold front crosses the region. Rainfall rates\nshould be light enough to preclude a flood risk. Fog and stratus\nis expected to develop overnight into early Monday morning with\nlow level moisture in place. This should dissipate Monday\nmorning, with dry weather then returning for most of the area. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nShortwave ridging and surface high pressure favors a drying\ntrend Monday into Tuesday, though enough moisture may linger to\noffer low probability afternoon thunderstorms that develop at\npeak diurnal heating.\n\nLong-range pattern favors renewed shortwave trough movement\nthrough the Ohio River Valley but with varying depths and\ntiming. If the pattern features a weaker trough farther north, \nnear daily precipitation chances may better be accompanied by \nsevere/flood hazards due to better moisture influx and limited \nairmass changing. A deeper trough pattern would result in less \nfrequent precipitation chances that limits the days of potential\nimpacts. It remains best to monitor forecast trends for\nWednesday into next weekend to better understand potential storm\ntiming and their associated risks.",
